"A leaf is green because it is full of chlorophyll. This is the pigment that catches sunlight so that the leaf can make food. There are other pigments in the leaf as well, yellow and orange ones called carotenoids. In summer you cannot see them, because there is so much green on top.": "695847d6d20f",
"A pigment gives a leaf its color.": "1e8ae74dcf82",
"As the green fades, the yellow and orange are still there, and now you can see them. That is all it takes to turn a tree gold.": "c7d12fd039ef",
"At last the tree grows a thin layer of cork across the base of each leaf stalk. This shuts off the water. The leaf dries, turns brown, and falls. The tree has not lost much. It has taken back what it could, and it will grow new leaves in spring.": "ef2a66e159f3",
"Bright days make the leaves redder.": "0f70b1587d0d",
"Chlorophyll does not last. Sunlight wears it out, and the tree has to keep making more. In autumn the days grow shorter and cooler, and the tree stops. It breaks the chlorophyll down and moves the useful parts back into its branches, to keep through the winter.": "0c1b11b8ab42",
"Cool nights help to make red leaves.": "a7a180db28be",
"In some parts of the world, whole hillsides turn yellow, orange, and red for a few weeks every autumn. It looks as if someone has painted the trees. But most of that color was not added in autumn. It was in the leaves all summer.": "4816e53ebb9e",
"In some parts of the world, whole hillsides turn yellow, orange, and red for a few weeks every autumn. It looks as if someone has painted the trees. But most of that color was not added in autumn. It was in the leaves all summer. A leaf is green because it is full of chlorophyll. This is the pigment that catches sunlight so that the leaf can make food. There are other pigments in the leaf as well, yellow and orange ones called carotenoids. In summer you cannot see them, because there is so much green on top. Chlorophyll does not last. Sunlight wears it out, and the tree has to keep making more. In autumn the days grow shorter and cooler, and the tree stops. It breaks the chlorophyll down and moves the useful parts back into its branches, to keep through the winter. As the green fades, the yellow and orange are still there, and now you can see them. That is all it takes to turn a tree gold. Red is different. Red comes from a third kind of pigment, and it is not in the leaf during the summer. Some trees make it in autumn from the sugar in their leaves. They make the most when the days are bright and the nights are cool but not freezing. That is why the same tree can be brilliant one year and dull the next. At last the tree grows a thin layer of cork across the base of each leaf stalk. This shuts off the water. The leaf dries, turns brown, and falls. The tree has not lost much. It has taken back what it could, and it will grow new leaves in spring.": "0aa2e4c74c80",
